## Judgment 2: session-census honesty — HONEST AGAINST THE TICK, GAMEABLE BY THE PRE-TICK TALKS (advisory A1)
  The census (`readdirSync(sessions).filter(.json).length`) is taken after both runTalk calls and before runTick.
  Nothing runTick writes or deletes can move it, so M13/M14-style tick routing cannot game it. The expected
  resume count is still NOT pinned, though. It is whatever the code under test left in sessions/ during the
  two runTalk calls, and the test knows it talked only to eng-lead. Two mutations of talk.ts's session store
  show this (my choice, one inflating the census and one deflating it):
    M16 census inflation: `writeSession(sessPath, {` -> on the modelOnly path, also write the same record to
        sessions/producer.json (cross-sella session bleed).
        Phase probe: tick = [v,resume, v,start, v,resume, v,start, v,start], so producer resumes eng-lead's session.
        harness-env alone: SURVIVED (exit=0 FAIL=0). Full npm test: SURVIVED (exit=0 FAIL=0 PASS=1916).
    M17 census deflation: after writeSession, `if (params.modelOnly && existing) rmSync(sessPath)` (a resume
        deletes its own session file).
        Phase probe: tick = 5x [v,start], which is M13's observable (eng-lead never resumes on the tick).
        Full npm test: SURVIVED (exit=0 FAIL=0 PASS=1922).
  Both are session-store bugs in talk.ts, not scrub or spawn-count bugs: every spawn still carries the exact
  projection, and the map is exact relative to the state on disk. My round-1 R2 said a count "derived from
  the session files present before the tick ... is enough", and the builder implemented that literally, so
  this is not a remedy defect and does not block. The handoff's summary "(1 resume for eng-lead, 4 starts)" is
  stronger than what is asserted. Nothing pins the 1 or eng-lead.
  Remedy (one line, harness-env.test.ts): before runTick, assert
    readdirSync(sessionsDir).filter(f => f.endsWith(".json")) deep-equals ["eng-lead.json"].
    This kills M16 and M17, and the existing census check then carries "1 resume, 4 starts" exactly.
  Class (expected value derived from state written by the code under test). I searched every readdirSync and
  sessions/ read in harness-env.test.ts, talk-boundary.test.ts and packages/shim/test/talk.test.ts, and
  this is the only instance. behaviour 4's other derived expectation, `added`, is pinned to 5. talk.test.ts
  checks that sessions/eng-lead.json exists but never that no other sella file exists, and never that it
  survives a resume, which is why M16/M17 also survive the full suite. Recommend folding both into W-056.

## Judgment 3: red 04 provenance — SOUND (one advisory note)
  studio/ci/reds/W-049/04.log: `# command: node scripts/w049-mutation-check.mjs M13`, exit 1, sella builder-b,
  tree dirty:f2415a01... The capture shows, in order: an empty pre-mutation diff of talk.ts, then the M13 hunk
  applied (index c54723a..7978581), then behaviour 4's run with exactly one FAIL (the new census check,
  resumeCount=0 startCount=5), then an empty post-revert diff, then "[mutation] ... exited 1 under M13". So the
  mutation was applied and reverted inside the captured command. The script is kept at
  scripts/w049-mutation-check.mjs.
  Base tree: I recomputed sourceTreeHash(f0d6221) = f2415a0114fa9aa652db7ca1fe9fe57a9dcc8dac, which is round 1's
  certified source tree. The only dirt at red time was the uncommitted test edit, which is test-first.
  Note (advisory, no action required): the script exits 1 both when the mutation is killed AND when it SURVIVES
  (after printing SURVIVED), so `bisellium red` would accept a survivor. For a record made with this script,
  the log text is the proof, not the exit code, and this log shows the FAIL. The re-record also replaced the
  original behaviour-4 red, which showed the scrub absent at runTalk/tick (env-projection FAILs). That red now
  exists only in history (f0d6221:studio/ci/reds/W-049/04.log). Behaviours 1-3's reds still show the scrub-absent red.

## Judgment 4: W-055 / W-056 scaffolds — ACCURATE
  W-055: "the timeline model field is absent for a real claude turn (envelope carries modelUsage, not a top-level
    model), so a canary model string can never fail the check that reads it." Matches round-1 Judgment 4.
  W-056: "talk-boundary.test.ts:246 and :341 accept the spawn map by fixed offset/inference rather than an exact
    per-call count." Matches round-1 Judgment 2. It is slightly compressed: :246 asserts presence, not a count
    of exactly 2, and M13 survives :341. That is adequate for a backlog scaffold. Add A1 to it.
  Both are backlog, with TODO sections (expected for a filed scaffold, not a spec).

